Fixed and committed as 6f5dfe3b with the title "Fixes #1015: …". When a piped yoyo -p run gets SIGTERM, it now writes the text it was holding, prints the (interrupted) note to stderr, and exits 143. Ctrl+C already did this and still exits 130.

Before the fix, I wrote the new test first and ran it against HEAD. stdout was [] (0 bytes) and the process died from the signal with no exit code, which is the bug.

What changed:
• **New file src/prompt/sigterm.rs:** it listens for SIGTERM only on runs whose text is being held (single-shot, stdout is a pipe). Terminal, REPL, `--print`, json and stream-json runs never register it, so SIGTERM still kills those immediately as before.
• **No swallowed signals:** once a handler is registered, SIGTERM no longer kills the process by itself. So the handler always ends the process with 143.
  • If the text loop is running, the handler passes it the signal. The loop writes out the held text, and if the process hasn't exited 5 seconds later, the handler exits it. A second SIGTERM skips the wait.
  • If no loop is running, it flushes stdout and exits right away.
• **One shared path for both signals:** the old Ctrl+C code moved into `interrupt::finish_interrupted_turn`, and both the Ctrl+C and SIGTERM branches call it instead of keeping two copies. `src/main.rs now gets its exit code from interrupted_exit_code()`: 143 for SIGTERM, 130 for SIGINT.
• Tests in tests/print_stdout_contract.rs:
  • SIGTERM mid-stream: stdout is exactly PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n, the note is on stderr, exit is 143.
  • SIGTERM during a bash sleep 30 tool call: the process exits with 143 within 10 seconds. On HEAD it died from the signal.
• **Positive control:** I forced the handler off and both new tests failed by name. I restored it in the same command and no sabotage marker is left.
• **One existing test needed updating:** the source check that counts gated stdout writes (`every_streamed_text_write_is_gated`) failed because the interrupt write moved into `interrupt.rs`. It now counts that file too and requires the total to stay 15, so it is no looser than before.
• **Docs:** one sentence in `docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md`. I also added an entry to `ARCHITECTURE.md` that names `evolve.sh`'s `timeout` kills as one of the affected callers.

Checks: cargo build, the full cargo test suite (0 failures), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo fmt -- --check and tests/harness_logic.sh all pass. src/prompt.rs is 3904 lines, under its recorded limit of 3906. I re-ran the assessment's probe, timeout -s TERM 12 yoyo -p "…" | wc -c, and it gave 43 bytes, up from 0. That was one real-model run, and I didn't check the exit code directly: the 124 it showed is timeout's own code, which it reports no matter how yoyo exits.

Two limits:
• On non-Unix systems nothing changes.
• A SIGTERM that arrives during startup, before the first prompt is read, still kills the process the default way.
